  "uT": 1.0,
  "summary": "Murphy's 20-minute frontal session for the Shiva system, which the vendor offers against 'lethargy, listlessness or mental fogginess' after too much temporal-lobe or one-sided stimulation. The hippocampal and amygdala signals run one after the other from still coils over both eyes with opposite polarity. The vendor states it 'treats both sides of the brain the same'.",
  "setting": "Only as needed after over-stimulation. The vendor's page offers tech support 'for more information'; it does not make contact a condition. Four coils on the forehead: Fp1 and AF3 above the left eye, Fp2 and AF4 above the right eye; the other four coils stay unused.",
  "schedule": "One 20-minute run; the vendor says it 'can be run twice, if necessary', and not three times.",
  "notes": "The vendor: 'This session is a 20-minute session that applies the hippocampal and amygdala signals, one after the other. It is suggested for use with only two or four coils. Set up the coils so that they have opposite polarities, and apply them with one or two coils over each eye.' (Murphy, mono_sdu.htm, 'A Frontal Lobe Session for the Shiva Neural System'). The page gives no other timing. This program puts the signal on the left pair (Fp1, AF3) and the reversed signal on the right pair (Fp2, AF4). Splitting the polarity by side is this project's reading of that sentence, and so are the site names. For the two-coil form, unplug AF3 and AF4. The coils stay still. One pass of 9.5 s: the hippocampal signal as a primed burst (one pulse, then four pulses 170 ms later, three times in 0.74 s, after Tsang 2004 p.5 [1017]) (0.84 s; the number of bursts is a project choice), 4 s of silence, the amygdala signal (0.69 s), 4 s of silence. Both are rebuilt from descriptions: the vendor's files are not published. The amygdala signal is the project's rising sweep (6 to 30 Hz) with the published point duration and length (230 points of 3 ms; Ryczko 2002 p.1 [592]). Its point values were never published as numbers, only as a small figure. The 3-ms points and the 4-s gaps follow the vendor's Shakti pages (rapid_&_high_speed_signals.htm). The cited Shiva pages give no field strength, only 'about as strong as the ones from a (land-line) telephone handset or a hair dryer' (shiva_infomed_consent.htm). This program's setting is the peak field 20 mm in front of each coil. The session rests on vendor statements only and is not a treatment for anything. Independent sham-controlled tests of God-Helmet-type fields found no field effect (Granqvist 2005, double-blind; Gendle & McGrath 2012, where the experimenter knew the condition). Do not exceed two runs; stop on distress. Not for people with epilepsy or a psychiatric condition.",
  "targets": ["vmpfc", "left-dlpfc", "right-dlpfc"],
  "source": "Shiva demo: A Frontal Lobe session (session to stop unwanted effects) https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/shiva/shiva_demo/mono_sdu.htm ; God Helmet session using amygdala and hippocampus signals (stopping unwanted effects) https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/god-helmet/koren_mono_sdu.htm ; Shakti demo: What are 'Rapid' and 'High-Speed' signals? (3-ms points, 4-s latency) https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/winshakti/demo/shakti_demo/rapid_&_high_speed_signals.htm ; Mood enhancement / feelgood, 8 Coil Shakti (signals one after the other with a 4-s latency) https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/winshakti/rotating/shakti_happiness.htm ; Shakti demo: Formal Discussion (about 9.6 s of field per minute) https://www.god-helmet.com/wp/winshakti/demo/shakti_demo/mono_proposal.htm ; Ryczko & Persinger 2002, Percept Mot Skills 95:592 (230-point burst-firing pattern) https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12434855/ ; Granqvist et al. 2005, Neurosci Lett 379:1 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15849873/ ; Gendle & McGrath 2012, Percept Mot Skills 114:217 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22582690/",
